scheme shows material delays, design uncertainty, and potential funding gaps","iso":"2026-09-23","paper_title":"Appendix B - CAPITAL FORECAST 202627","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61636/61636.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - CAPITAL FORECAST 202627","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61636/61636.pdf","headline":"Study phase cost estimates on Conigar and Northney flood risk schemes are higher than originally assumed","detail":"Cost estimates for early-stage study work on two major flood and habitat schemes have been reviewed and found higher than planned. Conigar's 2026/27 budget now shows \u00a3305k estimated costs versus \u00a350k existing approved budget. Northney shows \u00a3412k estimated costs versus \u00a370k. If study costs are escalating in early phases, further cost increases are likely as schemes progress to detailed design and delivery.","category":"capital","matters":"Cost inflation in early project stages indicates likely further overruns as schemes move through design and delivery, affecting the council's ability to deliver its capital programme within budget","figures":"Conigar \u00a37.95m lifetime (study estimate \u00a3305k); Northney \u00a320.75m lifetime (study estimate \u00a3412k)","quote":"Cost estimates for Study Stage have been reviewed and are higher than originally assumed. Aim to receive approval by November 2026.  2026/27 budget therefore shows existing approved budget of \u00a370k plus estimated costs for this f/y budget for the Study (412k).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - CAPITAL FORECAST 202627","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61636/61636.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation refurbishment costs are escalating due to material and contractor cost inflation","detail":"The council is refurbishing two temporary accommodation properties at Stockheath Road and London Road. Design estimates for Stockheath Road now predict works may exceed budget, with rising material and contractor costs cited as the cause. The council is seeking alternative estimates and considering a phased approach to contain costs. This affects the \u00a32.7m purchase budget and \u00a3448k fit-out budget.","category":"capital","matters":"A lender must monitor whether capital investments encounter cost pressures that could affect programme delivery and financial sustainability, particularly for housing services under pressure nationally","figures":"Purchase of TA \u00a32,700k lifetime, Fit out \u00a3448k lifetime","quote":"Based on one design company estimate predicted works for Stockheath Road may be higher than budgeted (London Road cost estimate expected by E/O Aug). Causes include the rise of material and contractor costs over recent years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - CAPITAL FORECAST 202627","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61636/61636.pdf","headline":"The Broadmarsh coastal defence scheme faces seven-month design delays with regulatory risks that could require additional funding","detail":"The Broadmarsh scheme (\u00a31.258m budget) experienced a seven-month delay to detailed design completion (from March 2026 to December 2026) due to Ground Investigation procurement delays and Flood Risk Activity Permit requirements. The council identifies two main risks: regulators may reject the proposed design approach requiring costlier alternatives, and the three-year construction deadline may be missed if construction funding is not secured. The project has no contingency allowance built in.","category":"capital","matters":"A lender needs confidence that major infrastructure projects will be delivered on schedule and budget; this scheme shows material delays, design uncertainty, and potential funding gaps","figures":"\u00a31,258 budget, 7 month delay, spend to date \u00a3479,600, estimated outturn \u00a31,039,655","quote":"The detailed design completion date had to move from March 2026 to December 2026 due to GI procurement and onsite delays, These were related to the necessity of Flood Risk Activity Permits (FRAP) for the investigations. This 7 month delay impacts the end date","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix B - CAPITAL FORECAST 202627","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61636/61636.pdf","headline":"The \u00a379.9m Eastoke flood and coastal erosion scheme cannot proceed without unsecured Environment Agency Grant in Aid funding","detail":"The Eastoke scheme is budgeted at \u00a379.907m over its lifetime. The FCERM7 business case is expected to be submitted in late summer 2026, with the outcome unclear until late autumn or early winter 2026. The council explicitly states the project cannot develop unless EA Grant in Aid funding is secured.","category":"capital","matters":"A lender must understand whether the council's capital programme depends on external funding that may not materialise, as this affects financial sustainability","figures":"\u00a379,907 lifetime budget; outcome unclear until late autumn/early winter 2026","quote":"Development of the project will be subject to securing Environment Agency Grant in Aid funding. If no funding is secured then the project will not progress.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-23","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Quarter 1 Finance and Performance Report","paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61634/61634.pdf","headline":"Financial sustainability and excessive financial pressure are identified as the council's highest-scoring corporate risk.","detail":"The corporate risk register highlights concerns regarding the council's financial sustainability, with the risk of excessive financial pressure identified as the highest-scoring risk. Mitigation measures include timely and accurate reporting of the forecast position, as reflected in this quarterly report.","category":"governance","matters":"When a council identifies financial sustainability as its highest-scoring risk, it signals management's awareness of severe financial distress.","figures":"Excessive financial pressure identified as highest-scoring risk on corporate risk register","quote":"The corporate risk register already highlights concerns regarding the Council's financial sustainability. The risk of excessive financial pressure remains the highest-scoring risk","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-07"},"name":"Havant","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Capital Financing Requirement forecast to rise 31% from \u00a321.7m to \u00a328.4m by 2029; council shifting to net borrower status","lead":"Material increase in planned capital spending and borrowing requirements signals a shift in council's financial strategy; affects debt sustainability, refinancing requirements, and medium-term funding dependency.","also":["24 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-23","n_findings":25,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"AppendixAAnnualTreasuryManagementOutturn2526","top_paper_url":"https://havant.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s61485/61485.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":93,"news_drafts_total":3}}